WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.800
A red blood cell that is nearly mature throws its transferrin receptors out of the

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:16.580
cell. They leave still sitting in membrane, on vesicles about 50 nanometres across.

3
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.120
A vesicle is a small sac wrapped in one membrane.

4
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:27.180
For years this was read as rubbish disposal, but could it also be a message?

5
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.800
First, where do these vesicles come from?

6
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:34.933
The answer starts inside the cell.

7
00:00:35.793 --> 00:00:41.593
The membrane of a compartment called an endosome buds inward, away from the cytosol,

8
00:00:41.913 --> 00:00:42.933
the cell's own fluid.

9
00:00:43.873 --> 00:00:47.373
It pinches off small vesicles into its own interior.

10
00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:50.133
These vesicles carry receptors.

11
00:00:51.173 --> 00:00:54.453
A compartment packed with them is a multivesicular body.

12
00:00:55.617 --> 00:01:01.017
Picture this compartment as a sorting room with two doors, one to the incinerator

13
00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:02.357
and one to the street.

14
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:07.577
If it fuses with a lysosome, its vesicles and their cargo are digested.

15
00:01:08.097 --> 00:01:12.437
But the same kind of compartment can move to the cell surface and fuse with the

16
00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:18.517
plasma membrane. Its vesicles are then outside the cell, intact, and those vesicles

17
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:24.877
are exosomes. The word exosome therefore names an origin: a vesicle that formed

18
00:01:24.877 --> 00:01:27.017
inside a multivesicular body.

19
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:30.867
The direction of budding fixes the geometry.

20
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:37.927
The bud forms away from the cytosol, so the inside of an exosome is a sample of

21
00:01:37.927 --> 00:01:42.567
cytosol. Its outer face is the face that was the outside of the cell.

22
00:01:43.027 --> 00:01:45.467
So a receptor keeps its orientation.

23
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:51.147
The part that sat outside the cell still faces outward, as it did on the plasma

24
00:01:51.147 --> 00:01:52.147
membrane.

25
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:58.110
Cells also release vesicles that bud straight outward from the plasma membrane,

26
00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.830
called microvesicles.

27
00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.150
They are a different kind of vesicle by origin.

28
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.330
But they have the same orientation, and they overlap in size.

29
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:13.270
The usual markers of small vesicles, the tetraspanins, are found on both kinds.

30
00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:19.290
So once both kinds are in the culture medium, no agreed test reliably tells them

31
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.290
apart.

32
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:27.340
An exosome is defined by where it formed, and size, markers and orientation do not

33
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.620
reliably show that.

34
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.960
Showing that a vesicle carries a message is a separate job, and it needs controls.

35
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.160
To call that vesicle an exosome, you must also show that it came from a

36
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.400
multivesicular body.

37
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.060
The field's own society urges authors to say extracellular vesicle unless they can

38
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.720
show where a vesicle was made.

39
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.200
So keep this rule: say exosome only when you can show where it formed.
